<ArticleLayout title={page.title} hreflang={hreflang} description={page.description} canonical={page.canonical} author={page.author} date={page.date} ogImage="/images/微信图片_20241027134849-1024x1024.jpg" bodyHtml={`<p>As a long-term <strong>fava bean supplier</strong> working closely with food manufacturers, plant-based protein developers, and bulk ingredient buyers, we are often asked a simple question:</p><blockquote><p><em>Are fava beans really as nutritious as people claim?</em></p></blockquote><p>The short answer is <strong>yes</strong>—but from a supplier’s perspective, nutrition facts are only meaningful when they are <strong>consistent, measurable, and applicable in real production environments</strong>.<br/>This article explains <strong>fava beans nutrition facts</strong> based on scientific evidence and our hands-on experience in supplying <strong>food-grade dried fava beans</strong> for commercial use.</p><hr/><h2>What Are Fava Beans?</h2><p><a href="/dinweys-bulk-broad-bean-new-production/">Fava beans</a> (<em>Vicia faba</em>), also known as broad beans, are one of the oldest cultivated legumes in the world. They are consumed both as <strong>fresh green beans</strong> and <strong>dried mature beans</strong>, with dried fava beans being the dominant form used in food manufacturing, plant protein processing, and bulk ingredient supply.</p><p>From a supply chain standpoint, <strong>dried fava beans</strong> are preferred because they offer:</p><ul><li>Stable nutritional composition</li><li>Longer shelf life</li><li>Higher protein density per unit weight</li></ul><hr/><h2>Fava Beans Nutrition Facts (Per 100g)</h2><p>Nutritional values can vary slightly depending on variety and processing, but the ranges below are well supported by analytical data and published research.</p><h3>Protein Content</h3><p>Fava beans are a <strong>high-protein legume</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Dried fava beans</strong>: approximately <strong>20–25 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, with some varieties reaching close to <strong>30% protein on a dry matter basis</strong></li><li><strong>Fresh fava beans</strong>: approximately <strong>8–10 g of protein per 100 g</strong></li></ul><p>From our experience supplying fava beans to food processors, this protein level makes them an excellent <strong>plant-based protein source</strong>, particularly suitable for:</p><ul><li>Vegetarian and vegan diets</li><li>Protein fortification in cereal-based foods</li><li>Alternative protein formulations</li></ul><p>Scientific studies confirm that fava bean protein has a <strong>balanced amino acid profile</strong> and good digestibility compared to many other legumes (Multari et al., 2015).</p><hr/><h3>Dietary Fiber</h3><p>Fava beans are also rich in <strong>dietary fiber</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>5–11 g of dietary fiber per 100 g</strong></li></ul><p>This fiber content contributes to:</p><ul><li>Improved intestinal motility</li><li>Cholesterol reduction</li><li>Prolonged satiety and glycemic control</li></ul><p>In commercial food applications, fiber-rich legumes like fava beans are frequently used in <strong>functional foods</strong> aimed at digestive health and weight management.</p><hr/><h3>Vitamins in Fava Beans</h3><p>Fava beans provide a broad range of vitamins, particularly <strong>B-complex vitamins</strong>, including:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)</li><li>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</li><li>Vitamin B6</li><li><strong>Folate (Vitamin B9)</strong></li><li>Vitamin C</li><li>Vitamin K</li></ul><p>Folate deserves special attention: it plays a critical role in <strong>DNA synthesis, red blood cell formation, and neural development</strong>, making fava beans nutritionally valuable for a wide range of populations (USDA, supported by peer-reviewed nutrition data).</p><hr/><h3>Mineral Profile</h3><p>Fava beans contain multiple essential minerals, such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium</strong> – supports bone health</li><li><strong>Iron</strong> – essential for hemoglobin synthesis and prevention of iron-deficiency anemia</li><li><strong>Potassium</strong> – helps maintain electrolyte balance and stable blood pressure</li><li><strong>Magnesium</strong> – involved in energy metabolism and muscle function</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong> – supports immune function and growth</li></ul><p>From a supplier’s quality-control perspective, mineral content stability is influenced by <strong>harvest maturity and drying methods</strong>, which is why standardized processing is critical for bulk supply.</p><hr/><h2>Antioxidants and Bioactive Compounds</h2><p>Beyond basic nutrition, fava beans contain several <strong>bioactive compounds</strong>, including:</p><ul><li>Polyphenols</li><li>Flavonoids</li></ul><p>These compounds exhibit <strong>antioxidant activity</strong>, helping to neutralize free radicals and potentially reduce the risk of chronic diseases such as cardiovascular disorders and metabolic syndrome (Boukid et al., 2019).</p><hr/><h2>Other Functional Components in Fava Beans</h2><p>Fava beans also contain naturally occurring compounds such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Phospholipids</strong></li><li><strong>Choline</strong></li><li><strong>L-DOPA (Levodopa)</strong></li></ul><p>L-DOPA is of particular scientific interest due to its role in <strong>dopamine synthesis</strong> and neurological function. While dietary intake alone is not therapeutic, studies confirm that fava beans are one of the richest natural food sources of this compound (Raina et al., 2014).</p><hr/><h2>Low Fat, High Nutritional Density</h2><p>Another advantage of fava beans is their <strong>low fat content</strong>, making them suitable for:</p><ul><li>Low-fat diets</li><li>Heart-health-oriented food products</li></ul><p>This combination—<strong>high protein, high fiber, low fat</strong>—is why fava beans are increasingly used in <strong>plant-based and clean-label formulations</strong>.</p><hr/><h2>Why Nutrition Facts Matter to Buyers</h2><p>From our role as a fava bean supplier, we see that buyers care less about theoretical nutrition values and more about:</p><ul><li>Batch-to-batch consistency</li><li>Dry matter protein stability</li><li>Suitability for processing (milling, extrusion, fermentation)</li></ul><p>Well-documented nutrition facts allow manufacturers to formulate products with <strong>predictable outcomes</strong>, which is essential for scaling production.</p><hr/><h2>Final Thoughts: Nutrition Is Only Valuable When Supply Is Reliable</h2><p>Scientific research clearly shows that <strong>fava beans are nutritionally excellent</strong>—rich in protein, dietary fiber, vitamins, minerals, and antioxidant compounds, while remaining naturally low in fat.</p><p>However, from a supplier’s perspective, these nutrition facts only translate into real value when fava beans are:</p><ul><li>Properly harvested</li><li>Carefully dried</li><li>Consistently graded and stored</li></ul><p>That is why nutrition data and supply-chain expertise must go hand in hand.</p><hr/><h2>FAQ</h2><h3><strong>1. Are fava beans a good source of protein?</strong></h3><p>Yes. Fava beans are considered a <strong>high-protein legume</strong>. Dried fava beans typically contain <strong>20–25 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, and in some varieties protein content can approach <strong>30% on a dry matter basis</strong>. This makes them an excellent plant-based protein source, especially for vegetarian and vegan diets.</p><hr/><h3><strong>2. How much protein is in fresh vs dried fava beans?</strong></h3><p>Fresh fava beans usually contain <strong>8–10 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, while dried fava beans are much more concentrated, providing <strong>20–25 g per 100 g</strong> due to lower moisture content. This is why dried fava beans are preferred in food manufacturing and bulk supply.</p><hr/><h3><strong>3. Are fava beans high in dietary fiber?</strong></h3><p>Yes. Fava beans contain approximately <strong>5–11 g of dietary fiber per 100 g</strong>, which supports digestive health, helps regulate cholesterol levels, and contributes to satiety. Their fiber content is one reason they are widely used in functional and health-oriented food products.</p><hr/><h3><strong>4. What vitamins are found in fava beans?</strong></h3><p>Fava beans are rich in <strong>B-complex vitamins</strong>, including <strong>vitamin B1, B2, B6, and folate (vitamin B9)</strong>. They also provide <strong>vitamin C and vitamin K</strong>, which play important roles in metabolism, red blood cell formation, immune function, and bone health.</p><hr/><h3><strong>5. What minerals do fava beans contain?</strong></h3><p>Fava beans contain several essential minerals such as <strong>calcium, iron, potassium, magnesium, phosphorus, and zinc</strong>. These minerals support bone health, oxygen transport in the blood, electrolyte balance, immune function, and overall metabolic health.</p><hr/><h3><strong>6. Do fava beans contain antioxidants?</strong></h3><p>Yes. Fava beans contain <strong>polyphenols and flavonoids</strong>, which are antioxidant compounds that help neutralize free radicals and may reduce the risk of chronic diseases, including cardiovascular and metabolic disorders.</p><hr/><h3><strong>7. Are fava beans low in fat?</strong></h3><p>Fava beans are naturally <strong>low in fat</strong>, making them suitable for low-fat diets and heart-health-focused food formulations. Their nutritional profile combines <strong>high protein, high fiber, and low fat</strong>, which is particularly attractive in plant-based food development.</p><hr/><h3><strong>8. Are fava beans suitable for food manufacturing and industrial use?</strong></h3><p>Yes. From a supplier’s perspective, dried fava beans are widely used in <strong>plant protein production, functional foods, snacks, and bulk ingredient applications</strong> due to their stable nutritional composition, high protein density, and good processing performance.</p><hr/><h3><strong>9. Do fava beans contain any special bioactive compounds?</strong></h3><p>Fava beans naturally contain compounds such as <strong>choline, phospholipids, and L-DOPA (levodopa)</strong>. L-DOPA is involved in dopamine synthesis and is one of the reasons fava beans are of scientific and nutritional interest.</p><hr/><h3><strong>10. Why is supplier quality important when evaluating fava beans nutrition facts?</strong></h3><p>Nutrition values are only reliable when the supply is consistent. Factors such as <strong>harvest maturity, drying method, moisture control, and storage conditions</strong> directly affect protein, fiber, and mineral stability. A reliable supplier ensures batch-to-batch nutritional consistency.</p><hr/><h2>References</h2><p>Boukid, F., Zannini, E., Carini, E., &amp; Vittadini, E. (2019). Pulses as functional ingredients: A review of processing effects on nutritional and functional properties. <em>Trends in Food Science &amp; Technology, 86</em>, 1–15.<br/><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tifs.2019.01.020" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_new">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tifs.2019.01.020</a></p><p>Multari, S., Stewart, D., &amp; Russell, W. R. (2015). Potential of fava bean (<em>Vicia faba</em>) as future protein supply to partially replace meat intake in the human diet. <em>Comprehensive Reviews in Food Science and Food Safety, 14</em>(5), 511–522.<br/><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.12146" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_new">https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.12146</a></p><p>Raina, A. P., Laskar, R. A., &amp; Khursheed, S. (2014). Quantification of L-DOPA in different cultivars of faba bean (<em>Vicia faba</em> L.). <em>Journal of Food Science and Nutrition, 2</em>(3), 89–95.<br/><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.93" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_new">https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.93</a></p>`} />
